>

  <h1>Sensitivity &amp; Specificity</h1>

  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> Sensitivity is the true-positive rate; specificity is the true-negative rate. In practice PPV and NPV matter more, because they account for how common the condition is.</p>
  <p>The two core measures of diagnostic accuracy — and two more you actually care about in practice.</p>

  <div class="card">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Meaning</th><th>What it answers</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Sensitivity</th><td>True positive rate</td><td>How well does it catch people who DO have the condition?</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Specificity</th><td>True negative rate</td><td>How well does it avoid false positives in people who DON'T?</td></tr>
        <tr><th>PPV</th><td>Positive predictive value</td><td>If it's positive, how likely is it a true positive? (depends on prevalence)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>NPV</th><td>Negative predictive value</td><td>If it's negative, how likely is it a true negative?</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Why PPV/NPV matter</h2>
  <p>
    A test with 95% sensitivity and 95% specificity can still give mostly false positives in a
    low-prevalence population — that's why confirmatory testing and prevalence context matter.
  </p>

  <p>See also <a href="/comparisons/rapid-test-vs-pcr/">rapid test vs PCR</a> and <a href="/comparisons/igg-vs-igm/">IgG vs IgM</a>.</p>

  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p><strong>Why do PPV and NPV matter more than sensitivity?</strong> They account for how common the condition is — a highly sensitive test can still be mostly wrong in a low-prevalence population.</p>
  </div>

